            <ArticleTitle>Irritant-induced asthma.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>140-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1097/ACI.0b013e32835143b8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="PURPOSE OF REVIEW" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To describe the recent insights into the definition, causes, natural outcome, and key elements of irritant-induced asthma (IIA) management.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RECENT FINDINGS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">IIA is a subtype of occupational asthma without immunologic sensitization and includes the typical reactive airway dysfunction syndrome (RADS) and a more gradual form called not-so-sudden IIA, when onset of asthma follows repeated low-dose exposure to irritants. The World Trade Center tragedy brought new insight in the understanding of IIA, suggesting that it can exhibit a prolonged interval between exposure and recognition of clinical symptoms and disease. Dimethyl sulfate has been recently reported to cause RADS and repeated diesel exhaust exposure to cause not-so-sudden IIA in patients who worked in a bus garage. Cleaning workers who are exposed to a large variety of irritants and sensitizers are especially at risk of occupational asthma and IIA.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUMMARY" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">IIA includes RADS and not-so-sudden IIA. Outcome of IIA is as poor as occupational asthma with sensitization. Treatment of IIA does not differ from standard asthma treatment, but high-dose vitamin D could be assessed further for possible therapeutic benefit.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
